$DIR/main.p:19:13: q holds a view of v (v.Slice(), line 17), which was changed by clear(s) at line 20, but the call deferred at line 19 uses it when the function returns
    q looks at memory that v owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    v.Slice() at line 17. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be
    used only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At line
    20 v was changed by clear(s), so the view may look at memory that was
    released, or at a value that now belongs to something else. The call
    deferred at line 19 keeps what q held there and runs when the function
    returns, after the deferred calls made after it (section 12.19).
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use q before line 20, or give v its new value only after the last use of q
    Or make the view again after line 20:
        q = v.Slice()
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
